Fix #1 — read the file as root (this is mandatory)

You must use sudo inside the command substitution.

Run this exactly:

PAIR_TOKEN=$(curl -s http://localhost:8080/api/pair/request \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "{\"device_name\":\"KDE-Linux\",\"hwid\":\"linux-refresh\",\"pubkey\":\"$(sudo grep -E '^QUOODLE_AGENT_PUBKEY_B64=' /etc/quoodle/secrets.env | cut -d= -f2)\"}" \
  | python3 -c "import sys, json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['pair_token'])")


Key difference:

$(sudo grep ...)


Without that → guaranteed failure.

Fix #2 — confirm it actually worked

Immediately check:

echo $PAIR_TOKEN


You should see something like:

ptk_abc123...


If it’s empty → stop, don’t continue.

Then generate the agent JWT

Only after PAIR_TOKEN exists:

NEW_AGENT_JWT=$(curl -s http://localhost:8080/api/pair/confirm \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $USER_JWT" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "{\"pair_token\":\"$PAIR_TOKEN\"}" \
  | python3 -c "import sys, json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['agent_jwt'])")


Verify:

echo $NEW_AGENT_JWT

Update the secrets file (must be sudo)
sudo sed -i "s|^QUOODLE_AGENT_JWT=.*|QUOODLE_AGENT_JWT=$NEW_AGENT_JWT|" /etc/quoodle/secrets.env

Restart the agent
sudo systemctl reset-failed quoodle-agent
sudo systemctl restart quoodle-agent

